/* Second selector matches the same element in light mode at higher specificity
   than the on-page furigana reset (`:is(.theme-light) .jpdb-reader-word rt` in
   reader-words-ocr.css), which would otherwise strip the outline. That reset
   avoids a muddy glow on light pages; player subtitles sit on dark footage and
   want the outline in both themes. Listed together so the shadow is declared once. */

.jpdb-subtitle-primary .jpdb-reader-furi,
:is(.jpdb-reader-theme-light, .yomu-page-theme-light) .jpdb-subtitle-primary .jpdb-reader-furi {
  /* Full opacity so the reading is the exact same colour as the caption text it
     annotates; the outline below keeps it legible over video. */
  color: currentColor;
  opacity: 1;
  /* Every radius and offset is em-relative, so the outline is the same SHAPE at
     every cue size. It used to be fixed pixels (0 1px 1px / 0 0 3px / 0 0 7px),
     which is what made kanji carrying a reading render dirtier than the
     okurigana beside them:

       * The clear space between the reading's ink and the kanji's ink is NOT a
         constant. It varies with the cue size, with the WORD, and with the
         engine. Measured across 続ける / 志す / 承る / 見る at 20/28/40/60px:
         0.185-0.345em in Blink, 0.345-0.489em in WebKit. At one 28px cue in
         Blink it is 0.246em for 続ける but 0.185em for 志す, because a wide
         four-kana reading sits lower over its kanji. An earlier version of this
         comment claimed a size-INVARIANT ~0.25em; that was measured on 続ける
         alone and is false in both directions. Nothing here may assume a fixed
         gap — the smoke measures it per word, per size, per engine.
       * A fixed 7px halo is 0.20em of the reading in a 60px cue but 0.43em in a
         28px cue and 0.60em in a 20px one. So the same declaration that sits
         inside the gap on a big cue overshoots it at the product default,
         painting a dark wash across the top of the kanji's band — and only
         there, because okurigana carries no reading. Measured reach into that
         band ran to 2.00x the whole gap in Blink and 1.50x in WebKit (worst on
         the wide readings, at 20px and 28px): the halo did not merely approach
         the kanji, it crossed onto it.

     Shrinking the radii alone is not enough, and neither is a plain px->em
     conversion: em radii calibrated to today's 28px look are 0.43em at EVERY
     size, which measurably makes 40px and 60px cues worse than they are now.

     The reach has to fit the gap at all sizes, and the ink lost to that has to
     come back WITHOUT reaching further down. Depth, not width, is the lever: a
     tight layer repeated stacks alpha in the one or two pixel rows that hug the
     glyph, where the eye reads the edge, while its reach stays that same tight
     radius. So the downward half is four short layers (0.022em x2, 0.045em x2)
     rather than one wide one, and only the upward half — which paints over open
     video, not over the kanji — is allowed a soft 0.24em atmosphere.

     An interim version of this fix pushed nearly all the mass upward and left a
     single 0.022em layer pointing down. That cured the wash but spent the
     reading's own downward legibility, which is the thing this outline exists
     for: glyph-vs-outline contrast BENEATH the kana over a bright frame fell
     14.59 -> 4.96 at the 28px default and 11.29 -> 3.31 at 20px in Blink. The
     stacked downward layers buy that back without buying back the reach.

     Measured, per direction, at 20/28/40/60px in both engines: every direction
     at every size in both engines is now at or above BOTH the owner-tuned pixel
     build and that interim version — e.g. Blink 28px down 14.59 (pixel build)
     / 4.96 (interim) / 17.94 (here); WebKit 20px down 3.25 / 4.23 / 7.33.
     Downward reach is 0.043-0.172em across those four words. Stated against the
     room each one actually has, it spends at most 0.67x the clear space — that
     worst case is 志す at 28px in Blink, where the whole clear band is only
     THREE device pixel rows and the outline occupies two of them, still leaving
     a clear row before the kanji's first ink. The wash it lays on the lower half
     of that space and the top of the kanji's own band is <=0.20 luminance,
     against <=3.10 for the pixel build. Verified by
     scripts/subtitle-furigana-outline-scale-smoke.mjs in Blink and WebKit. */
  text-shadow:
    0 .022em .035em var(--subtitle-outline, var(--jpdb-reader-video-outline)),
    0 .022em .035em var(--subtitle-outline, var(--jpdb-reader-video-outline)),
    0 .045em .05em var(--subtitle-outline, var(--jpdb-reader-video-outline)),
    0 .045em .05em var(--subtitle-outline, var(--jpdb-reader-video-outline)),
    0 -.02em .075em var(--subtitle-outline, var(--jpdb-reader-video-outline)),
    0 -.02em .075em var(--subtitle-outline, var(--jpdb-reader-video-outline)),
    0 -.05em .1em var(--subtitle-outline, var(--jpdb-reader-video-outline)),
    0 -.13em .24em var(--jpdb-reader-video-shadow-heavy);
}

